  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how human activities can trigger or amplify secondary earthquake hazards such as tsunamis, liquefaction, landslides, and flooding. It is organized around four broad drivers—hydrological changes, movement of earth, ecosystem changes, and impacts of anthropogenic climate change—with arrows showing cause-and-effect links.\n\nA central message is that many land and water management actions alter physical conditions that increase hazard susceptibility. In the hydrological group, irrigation, water-table changes, river-channel modification, and groundwater withdrawal are linked to effects such as subsidence and related flooding or liquefaction. In the earth-movement group, land reclamation, removal of coastal abiotic systems, and slope modification or road cuts appear to promote tsunami, landslide, or subsidence-related risks.\n\nThe climate-change section shows relative sea-level rise, extreme rainfall, changes in precipitation, drought, forest fire, and ocean warming/acidification as processes that feed into these hazard pathways. The ecosystem-change panel highlights deforestation, abandonment of cultivated land, removal of coastal vegetation, and destruction of coral reefs, which seem to reduce natural protection and thereby worsen landslide, flooding, or tsunami impacts.\n\nOverall, the diagram emphasizes that anthropogenic environmental change can cascade into multiple earthquake-related hazards through interconnected pathways.",
